  "summary": "Refined Pahari school known for its lyrical quality, subtle colors, and focus on romantic themes and nature.",
  "description": "Characterized by delicate brushwork, soft pastel colors, and naturalistic landscapes with flowing rivers and trees; developed under local patronage with strong influence from Vaishnavite poetry.",
  "culturalContext": "The roots of the Pahari miniature style—a living tradition still active today—trace back to the migration of Gujarati artist families to the Punjab Hill States in the late 16th century. Initially settling at Nurpur during the reign of Raja Bas Dev (r. 1580–1613), these artist lineages played a foundational role in bringing early North Indian and popular Mughal artistic conventions to the region before spreading to major hill centers including Chamba and Guler.",
